  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_theory_activity",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory: Predicting Molecular Geometry",
    "description": "An interactive AR lab exploring VSEPR (Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion) theory. Students learn how electron pairs around a central atom arrange to minimize repulsion, discover the five fundamental electron-domain geometries, distinguish electron-domain geometry from molecular geometry, understand why lone pairs occupy more angular space, and apply VSEPR predictions to real molecules. The lab progresses through narrated demonstrations of the minimum-repulsion principle, comparative analysis of molecular shapes, and interactive prediction challenges.",
    "workplace": "vsepr_lab_workplace",
    "start": "step_1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "Welcome to the VSEPR theory lab! VSEPR stands for Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion. The central idea is simple: electrons in bonding pairs and lone pairs around a central atom repel one another, and molecules arrange themselves in three-dimensional space to place these electron domains as far apart as possible, minimizing repulsion. This lab will show you how to predict molecular geometry starting from a Lewis structure. A central atom appears before you; to its side, a reference panel shows a Lewis structure of water (flat, on paper) and reminds you how to count electron domains: each single bond, double bond, triple bond, or lone pair counts as one domain. Once you understand the setup, click 'Next' to see the repulsion principle in action."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "animation",
              "poi": "nucleus_center"
            },
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_water",
              "poi": "lewis_diagram_spot"
            },
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vsepr_intro_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "domain_position_1"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2_minimum_repulsion"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_minimum_repulsion",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Minimum Repulsion Principle: Building Domain Geometries",
          "description": "Watch as electron domains (rendered as lobed clouds extending from the central atom) are added one by one. Two domains repel and settle 180 degrees apart in a **linear** arrangement. A third domain is added and all three rearrange into a **trigonal planar** configuration at 120 degrees apart. A fourth produces a **tetrahedral** geometry at 109.5 degrees. As each domain is added, faint repulsion vectors appear briefly during the rearrangement, then fade as the geometry stabilizes. Each settled configuration has a name that describes its electron-domain geometry. You can rotate the molecule freely to see it from any angle. When you're ready to see the complete catalog of all five electron-domain geometries, click 'Next'."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "repulsion_animation_2to3",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "repulsion_animation_3to4",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "repulsion_animation_4to5",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_water"
            }
          ]
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_summary_panel",
              "poi": "geometry_summary_spot"
            },
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "repulsion_animation_5to6",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_complete_geometries"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_complete_geometries",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Five Electron-Domain Geometries",
          "description": "Here is the complete catalog of electron-domain geometries. Two domains arrange **linear** at 180\u00b0. Three domains arrange **trigonal planar** at 120\u00b0. Four domains arrange **tetrahedral** at 109.5\u00b0. Five domains arrange **trigonal bipyramidal** with three equatorial domains at 120\u00b0 and two axial domains at 90\u00b0 to the equatorial plane. Six domains arrange **octahedral** with all neighbors at 90\u00b0. A summary panel to your side displays all five geometries side by side, showing their domain counts (2, 3, 4, 5, 6) and characteristic angles. Study this carefully, as these are the foundation for predicting any molecular shape. Rotate the molecules to view them from every angle. When you're confident in recognizing these five geometries, click 'Next' to learn how lone pairs change the picture."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "domain_position_1"
            },
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "domain_position_2"
            },
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "domain_position_3"
            },
            {
              "target": "demo_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "domain_position_4"
            },
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_summary_panel",
              "poi": "geometry_summary_spot"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_model",
              "poi": "methane_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ammonia_model",
              "poi": "ammonia_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_model",
              "poi": "water_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_summary_panel"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4_electron_vs_molecular_geometry"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_electron_vs_molecular_geometry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Electron-Domain Geometry vs. Molecular Geometry",
          "description": "This step introduces a crucial distinction. **Electron-domain geometry** describes how all electron domains (bonding and lone pairs) arrange in space. **Molecular geometry** describes the shape traced by the atoms alone\u2014lone pairs are not 'seen' because they have no nucleus attached. Before you are three molecules based on a tetrahedral electron-domain geometry. First, methane (CH\u2084): all four domains are bonding pairs (solid bonds to hydrogen atoms). Both electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry are **tetrahedral**. Next, replace one hydrogen with a lone pair to form ammonia (NH\u2083). The electron-domain geometry is still **tetrahedral** (all four domains still arrange tetrahedrally), but the molecular geometry is now **trigonal pyramidal** because only three atoms form the visible shape. Finally, replace a second hydrogen with another lone pair to form water (H\u2082O). Electron-domain geometry remains **tetrahedral**, but molecular geometry is now **bent**. The underlying electron arrangement has not changed; only which domains are bonds versus lone pairs has changed."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label",
              "text": "CH\u2084 Methane: Tetrahedral / Tetrahedral"
            },
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label",
              "text": "NH\u2083 Ammonia: Tetrahedral / Trigonal Pyramidal"
            },
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label",
              "text": "H\u2082O Water: Tetrahedral / Bent"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "reference_panel_location",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lone_pair_asymmetry_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_lone_pair_angular_space"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_lone_pair_angular_space",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lone Pair Angular Space and Bond-Angle Deviations",
          "description": "Why do bond angles change when a lone pair replaces a bonding pair? The answer lies in the angular space each domain occupies. **Lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs.** A lone pair is held only by the central atom's nucleus, so the electron cloud spreads out more and repels the neighboring bonding domains more strongly. In methane (CH\u2084), all four domains are bonding pairs, so the H-C-H angle is a perfect **109.5\u00b0**. In ammonia (NH\u2083), one domain is a lone pair; because it takes up more space, it compresses the three H-N-H bonding angles to approximately **107\u00b0**. In water (H\u2082O), two domains are lone pairs; they compress the H-O-H angle further to approximately **104.5\u00b0**. The electron-domain arrangement stays tetrahedral, but the bonding angles shrink as lone pairs take up more angular real estate. You can toggle a view that overlays all three molecules at the central atom to directly compare how the H-X-H angle decreases as more lone pairs are added."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label",
              "text": "CH\u2084: H-C-H = 109.5\u00b0"
            },
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label",
              "text": "NH\u2083: H-N-H \u2248 107\u00b0"
            },
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label",
              "text": "H\u2082O: H-O-H \u2248 104.5\u00b0"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "interactive_molecule_set",
              "type": "primitive",
              "augmentation": "label"
            }
          ]
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_gallery_area",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "molecule_gallery_co2",
              "poi": "linear_spot"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_gallery_area",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "molecule_gallery_bf3",
              "poi": "planar_spot"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_gallery_area",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_model",
              "poi": "tetrahedral_spot"
