          "description": "Before you is a gallery of real molecules spanning all five electron-domain geometries. **CO\u2082** (carbon dioxide) exhibits **linear** geometry with two bonding domains and no lone pairs on the central carbon atom. **BF\u2083** (boron trifluoride) exhibits **trigonal planar** geometry with three bonding domains. **CH\u2084** (methane) exhibits **tetrahedral** geometry. **PF\u2085** (phosphorus pentafluoride) exhibits **trigonal bipyramidal** geometry with all five domains as bonding pairs and no lone pairs. **SF\u2086** (sulfur hexafluoride) exhibits **octahedral** geometry with all six domains as bonding pairs. **XeF\u2084** (xenon tetrafluoride) is more complex: the central xenon has four bonding domains and two lone pairs, for a steric number of six (octahedral electron-domain geometry), but the molecular geometry is **square planar** because the lone pairs occupy the axial positions. You can approach each molecule and see its Lewis structure alongside its 3D structure. Observe how the count of bonding domains and lone pairs around the central atom determines both the electron-domain geometry and the resulting molecular shape."
