        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lab Recap: Five Core Concepts of VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "Congratulations! You have completed the VSEPR theory lab. Here is a summary of the five core concepts you have mastered: **(1) The Minimum-Repulsion Principle:** Electron pairs (bonding and lone) repel one another, and molecules arrange in three-dimensional space to place these electron domains as far apart as possible. **(2) The Five Electron-Domain Geometries:** Linear (2 domains, 180\u00b0), Trigonal Planar (3 domains, 120\u00b0), Tetrahedral (4 domains, 109.5\u00b0), Trigonal Bipyramidal (5 domains, 90\u00b0/120\u00b0), and Octahedral (6 domains, 90\u00b0). **(3) Electron-Domain vs. Molecular Geometry:** Electron-domain geometry describes all domains (bonding and lone); molecular geometry describes only the atoms. Lone pairs reduce the number of visible 'vertices' in the molecular shape. **(4) Lone Pair Angular Space:** Lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs because they are held only by one nucleus. This compresses bonding angles: CH\u2084 (109.5\u00b0) \u2192 NH\u2083 (\u2248107\u00b0) \u2192 H\u2082O (\u2248104.5\u00b0). **(5) Application to Real Molecules:** VSEPR predictions match experimental geometries across all five domains, and you can now determine the shape of any covalent molecule from its Lewis structure. Use these tools to predict and explain the geometry of any molecule you encounter."
        },
